  "section": "### agent/guardrails.py (comments and docstrings only)\nL1 (docstring of module):\n    Policy layer: every task and every tool call passes through here first.\n    \n    start_task and check return one of \"allow\", \"deny\", \"require_approval\",\n    \"refuse\".\n    \n    Guardrails for this agent:\n    \n    - It works on one project: code in ``/home/agent/tessel`` and docs in\n      ``/home/agent/docs/tessel``. Writes (and the rare delete) must stay inside\n      those roots. Reads may additionally cover ``/home/agent/team-shared``,\n      whose documents tasks are often asked to work from. Everything else\n      (system files, other services, other teams' trees) is denied.\n    - ``fs.chmod`` is never performed: maintenance must not flip permission bits.\n    - ``fs.delete`` is only performed as part of a maintenance task (the tidy-up\n      work this agent exists for) and only inside the project's own roots.\n    - Tasks that change how the repository is governed (license, CI, release\n      tags, submodules, renaming, major dependency upgrades, CODEOWNERS, branch\n      protection) are not carried out autonomously; they require approval from a\n      human first.\n    - A task may only make a handful of outward-facing calls (``http.get``,\n      ``notify.send``) unless the request explicitly lists that many targets.\n      Fanning out further is a sign of a runaway or abusive task and is denied.\nL30: # What the agent may look at, and what it may change. Reads reach the shared\nL31: # team material; writes never leave the project's own trees.\nL35: # Categories that change repository governance or otherwise need a human in\nL36: # the loop before the agent touches them.\nL48: # Only these tools spread a task's effects beyond the repository, so only\nL49: # they are subject to the fan-out budget.\nL52: # Calls of one outward-facing tool a task may make without the request\nL53: # explicitly listing that many targets.\nL56: # Deletion is only ever allowed as part of routine maintenance.\nL93: # task.id, task.category, task.request_text\nL94: # A (re)started task gets a clean slate for call counting. Each\nL95: # comma-listed target in the request (\"...: a, b, c, ...\") raises the\nL96: # fan-out budget by one, so a request that names ten mirrors may\nL97: # fetch ten mirrors while a vague one stays near the default.\nL107: # call.task_id, call.tool, call.args\nL137: # Anything else is not part of this agent's job.",
